# Pending human-applied patch — issue #841
#
# WHAT: neutralizes the two archaeology references in the guard's own comments —
#       guard.ts's file-header ("Ported from the predecessor project's guard.py...") and
#       guard-hook.ts's FAIL-CLOSED rationale comment ("a divergence from the predecessor
#       project which fails open"). Both keep the real constraint they document (guard.ts
#       implements a generic, application-agnostic safety mechanism; guard-hook.ts fails
#       closed on any error) and drop the private predecessor-repo framing. No behavior
#       change — comment-only.
#
# WHY IT MATTERS: #841 (public-launch hygiene) scrubs references to the project's private
#       predecessor from production code and user-facing docs — a user browsing the shipped
#       repo has no access to that private repo, and a comment pointing at it only confuses.
#       See #841's PR body for the full file-by-file classification table.
#
# WHY IT IS A PATCH AND NOT A COMMIT: `guard.ts` and its hook wiring (`guard-hook.ts`) are
#       human-merge-only (docs/security.md "Human-merge-only paths") — the guard protects
#       itself from producer edits, enforced by the guard, not by convention. An autonomous
#       worker's write to either file is denied at the tool level.
#
# APPLY (from the repo root), then delete this file:
#       git apply docs/patches/841-guard-archaeology-scrub.patch
#       git rm docs/patches/841-guard-archaeology-scrub.patch
#
diff --git a/engine/src/guard/guard.ts b/engine/src/guard/guard.ts
--- a/engine/src/guard/guard.ts
+++ b/engine/src/guard/guard.ts
@@ -5,12 +5,11 @@
 // "Host-delegated capability management" section (DR #616). Pure function: zero IO, zero
 // deps, deterministic.
 //
-// Ported from the predecessor project's guard.py. We port the *generic
-// safety mechanism* — command tokenizing, fragment splitting, exec-prefix stripping,
-// opaque-construct fail-closed detection, and the gh-overreach category (the worker, a
-// PR *producer*, must never merge/approve/release) — plus a Write-path protection for the
-// guard's own boundary files. The predecessor project's application-specific categories are
-// intentionally NOT ported (CLAUDE.md: "port the generic logic, not application-specific behavior").
+// Implements the *generic safety mechanism* — command tokenizing, fragment splitting,
+// exec-prefix stripping, opaque-construct fail-closed detection, and the gh-overreach category
+// (the worker, a PR *producer*, must never merge/approve/release) — plus a Write-path
+// protection for the guard's own boundary files. Application-specific categories are
+// intentionally not included (CLAUDE.md: "port the generic logic, not application-specific behavior").
 //
 // Decision order for a Bash command:
 //   1. split into fragments by shell chain operators; $()/`` substitutions recurse.
diff --git a/engine/src/guard/guard-hook.ts b/engine/src/guard/guard-hook.ts
--- a/engine/src/guard/guard-hook.ts
+++ b/engine/src/guard/guard-hook.ts
@@ -1,7 +1,7 @@
 // PreToolUse hook adapter. Wires the pure `guardDecision` to Claude Code's hook protocol:
 // reads the hook event JSON on stdin, emits a `permissionDecision: deny` on a BLOCK.
 //
-// FAIL-CLOSED (PLAN requirement, a divergence from the predecessor project which fails open): any error —
+// FAIL-CLOSED (PLAN requirement): any error —
 // malformed JSON, an unexpected payload shape, or the guard throwing — yields a DENY, not
 // a silent allow. A safety hook that can be disabled by feeding it garbage is not a
 // safety hook. The pure mapping (`hookResponse`) is offline-testable; only `main()` does IO.
